{#
    Example: the "showing results for ..." line above a corrected search.

    Include it at the top of your search result page:

        {% include "@foun10EasySearch/frontend/correction.html.twig" %}

    Small, and the one piece of markup a typo-tolerant search really should not
    ship without. When the engine corrects a query it answers for a word the
    customer did not type, and saying so is the difference between a search that
    looks clever and one that looks broken - especially when the correction is
    wrong, which is when the escape hatch below matters.

    Only rendered on the search page, because only SearchController carries the
    correction.
#}
{% block foun10_easysearch_correction %}
{% if oView.getFoun10SearchCorrection() %}
    {% set correction = oView.getFoun10SearchCorrection() %}

    <p class="foun10-correction">
        {% if correction.isApplied() %}
            {#
                The search was answered for the corrected word. Say which one,
                and offer the original back - a customer who really meant the
                unusual spelling has one click to insist on it.
            #}
            {{ translate({ ident: "FOUN10_EASYSEARCH_CORRECTION_APPLIED" }) }}
            <strong>{{ correction.getCorrected() }}</strong>

            <a class="foun10-correction__original"
               href="{{ oView.getFoun10UncorrectedSearchUrl() }}"
               rel="nofollow">
                {{ translate({ ident: "FOUN10_EASYSEARCH_CORRECTION_INSTEAD" }) }}
                <strong>{{ correction.getOriginal() }}</strong>
            </a>
        {% else %}
            {#
                The typed word found something, but a near one would find more.
                An offer rather than a correction, so the current result stays.
            #}
            {{ translate({ ident: "FOUN10_EASYSEARCH_CORRECTION_SUGGESTED" }) }}
            <a href="{{ oViewConf.getSelfActionLink() }}cl=search&amp;searchparam={{ correction.getCorrected()|url_encode }}">
                <strong>{{ correction.getCorrected() }}</strong>
            </a>
        {% endif %}
    </p>
{% endif %}

{#
    Deliberately no headline of its own. The theme already writes one - "40 hits
    for X" - and a second line saying the same thing is noise. This partial adds
    only what the theme cannot know: that the results belong to a different word
    than the one typed.

    If you do want the headline to name the corrected term, the value is
    oView.getFoun10EffectiveSearchParam(): after an applied correction that is
    the corrected word, while the theme's own getSearchParamForHtml() still
    returns what was typed - right for the input field, wrong beside a hit
    count, which is what makes "294 hits for bluse" read as a lie when bluse
    itself found nothing.
#}
{% endblock %}
